void *circ_queue_write_pos(circ_queue_t q, uint16_t *contig,
		uint16_t *avail) {
	void *contents = q->contents;
	uint16_t wr_head = q->write_head;
	uint16_t rd_tail = q->read_tail;
	if (contig) {
		if (rd_tail <= wr_head) {
			int16_t offset = 0;
			if (rd_tail != 0) {
				offset = -1;
			}
			*contig = q->num_elem - wr_head + offset;
		} else {
			*contig = rd_tail - wr_head - 1;
		}
	}
	if (avail) {
		if (rd_tail <= wr_head) {
			*avail = q->num_elem - wr_head + rd_tail - 1;
		} else {
			*avail = rd_tail - wr_head - 1;
		}
	}
	return contents + wr_head * q->elem_size;
}

comment:
Get a pointer to the current queue write position. This position is unavailable to the reader and may be filled in with the desired data without respect to any synchronization. No guarantee that `circ_queue_advance_write` will succeed, though.

Alternatively, in `*contig`, we return the number of elements that can be filled in. We guarantee that you can `circ_queue_advance_write_multi` that many. You can also always write one element, but no guarantees about advancing the write position.

@param[in] q Handle to circular queue.
@param[out] contig Number of elements available for contiguous write.
@param[out] avail Number of elements available before a reader frees up more space. Includes wraparound/non-contiguous elements.
@returns Position for new data to be written (of size `elem_size`).

Availability (`avail`) is the number of elements to the end of the buffer. Cannot advance to the beginning because we'd catch our tail. Only when the tail is perfectly wrapped. `rd_tail > wr_head`. `wr_head` cannot advance to meet `tail`, so subtract one. To the end of the buffer, to `rd_tail`, subtract one. Otherwise, just to one before `rd_tail`.

